    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d116">

                <pb facs="0261" n="167" sameAs="frus:frus1930v03/pg167"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.6812a/10</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Greek Minister</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Simopoulos</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-06-06">June 6, 1930</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of May 19, 1930 (numbered 898),<anchor n="6" xml:id="d116-f1"/> by
                    which you communicated to me the gratifying information that your Government is
                    ready to conclude a treaty of arbitration and a treaty of conciliation with the
                    Government of the United States of America in accordance with the drafts thereof
                    which were transmitted to your Legation in the Department’s note of April 23,
                    1928.</p>
                <p>Understanding that it is your wish that the two treaties be done in both the
                    English and French languages, I am enclosing for your consideration French
                    equivalents of the English drafts of the two treaties.<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d116-f2"/> These follow, <hi rendition="#italic">mutatis
                        mutandis</hi>, the French text of the treaties of arbitration and
                    conciliation concluded between the United States and <pb facs="0262" n="168"
                        xml:id="pg168"/> Rumania on March 1 [<hi rendition="#italic">21</hi>],
                        1929.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d116-f3"/> If these be satisfactory to you, I
                    shall, upon being so informed, direct the preparation of the two treaties for
                    signature and be happy to fix a time convenient to you for their signatures.</p>
                <p>Should you deem it desirable to suggest any changes in the French text of the
                    treaties, the Department will be glad to consider them.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. P.
                            Cotton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
